WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Alerts are useful only when they lead to action.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:23.000
A sovereign AI monitoring system should identify conditions that require human attention, such as service-down states, repeated model load failures, rising timeout rates, queue backlogs, storage exhaustion, retrieval connector failures, unusual request volume, runaway jobs, or policy exceptions.

3
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.000
Anomalies can reveal operational drift.

4
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:43.780
A sudden increase in requests, unexpected data source access, rapid storage growth, repeated retries, new error patterns, or a sharp change in hardware pressure may indicate misconfiguration, misuse, an integration loop, or a workflow that has outgrown its original design.

5
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.400
Keep the analysis defensive and focused on safe administration.

6
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.280
Alert quality matters.

7
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.900
If thresholds are too quiet, teams learn about problems from users or incidents.

8
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.080
If thresholds are too noisy, teams learn to ignore the alert channel.

9
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:05.020
Start with high-value signals, route them to the right owner, and tune them with real operating experience.

10
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.420
Every important alert should have a clear investigation path.

11
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.920
The alert should link to relevant dashboards, logs, traces, runbooks, owners, and recent changes.

12
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:21.420
It should explain what condition triggered, what impact may exist, and what first steps are reasonable.

13
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.900
Incident signals should also respect privacy.

14
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.320
Alert payloads do not need full prompts, outputs, or sensitive data by default.

15
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:38.700
They need enough context to start a responsible investigation without spreading protected information through notification systems.
